WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.880
50 years ago, external hard drives were the
size of refrigerators.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:09.830
Today, they squeeze a lot more memory into
drives the size of chocolate bars.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.140
On the hard drive assembly line.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.200
There's not a speck of dust.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:20.400
An air filtering system sucks particles out
of the air to keep them from contaminating

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.800
the drive and compromising its function.

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.760
Production starts with the motor base.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.880
An employee installs a voice coil magnet.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:34.830
It's one of two that will move the drive
heads to write information on rotating disks.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:39.360
They're called voice coils because they
operate on the same principle as

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:45.850
loudspeakers. Next, a robot places one of
the hard drive disks in the motor base.

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:52.700
A camera snaps a photo to send information
to guide the robot so it positions the disc.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:01:00.440
Exactly. Another robot places a round metal
spacer at the center of the disc, again

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.960
relying on the camera's eye for guidance.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:07.640
It sets a second disc on top of the spacer.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:15.300
This is a two disc hard drive, but a drive
can have several depending on the amount of

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.470
storage needed.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.670
A robot locks the discs into place with a
stainless steel clamp.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:29.810
This next part is a plastic ramp.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:36.380
The ramp is a place to park the disk, drive
heads when they're not engaged in reading and

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:45.940
writing data. Those tiny heads have been
preassembled to one end of the head

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:52.930
arm. They attach the other end of the arm to
the lower voice coil in the base and then

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:55.300
situate the second voice coil above it.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:04.010
To maintain its squeaky clean interior.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.800
A robot inserts a thin cotton filter.

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:13.910
This filter will capture dust particles in
the event that some infiltrate the hard

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:20.270
drive. A robot now places a metal cover on
the other side of the drive.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:31.660
Another robot then screws the cover to the
motor base.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:41.160
With the inner workings now encased, the
hard drive can be moved out of the air

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.870
filtered section of the factory.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:49.190
A worker collects the hard drive assemblies
and slots them into a tote box.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:56.390
Once the circuit board has been added, they
transfer the hard drives to a test chamber

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.560
behind the closed door of the chamber.

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.750
A robot swings into action.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:10.800
It plucks a hard drive from the tote box and
plugs it into a test cell to confirm that

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:18.440
it's functional. Outside employees go about
their daily business as the robot

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:21.830
methodically tests each hard drive in the
tote boxes.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:29.610
Once a tote of drives is confirmed to be
operational, the chamber ejects them and a

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.100
worker then does a visual inspection of each
one.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:37.080
She looks for scratches or other damage that
could hinder its function.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:43.290
There's another slightly different system
for testing in this one.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:48.840
The employee places each drive on a conveyor
that delivers it to a robot tester.

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:57.310
Having two tester systems ensures that if
one goes down, they still have another one to

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:02.830
carry on. After all, like humans, robots are
not infallible.

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:10.520
At the packing station.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.310
Now, there's one last visual inspection.

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:18.560
The employee scans the drive's serial number
so its production can be traced in the event

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:19.850
of a problem later.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:22.190
She then seals it in plastic.

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:30.020
This hard drive is now ready to accept data
and ensure that someone doesn't totally lose

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.510
it if their computer drive crashes.

